      "id": 1,
      "text": "Igleside’s roots trace back to the 1846 Rancho Canada de Guadalupe land grant that once covered the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://sanfrancisco.wiki/a/Ingleside",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The neighborhood’s name echoes its historic horse‑racing past, where the Ingleside racetrack drew jockeys, trainers, and owners.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://sanfrancisco.wiki/a/Ingleside",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Victorian and Edwardian homes line Ingleside’s streets, showcasing the architectural styles that define its historic character.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://sanfrancisco.wiki/a/Ingleside",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Mount Davidson, San Francisco’s highest natural point, sits just north of Ingleside, shaping the neighborhood’s skyline.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://sanfrancisco.wiki/a/Ingleside",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The K Ingleside light‑rail line, which opened in 1918, was the first to use the Twin Peaks Tunnel and still serves Ingleside today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
